course: dictionary
lesson: 12160
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can tell which meaning the writer wants by reading the words around the word. «The hotel gave us complimentary breakfasts.» The word hotel and the idea of a gift point to free, so complimentary means given without charge.
explain: «Her teacher was very complimentary about my drawing.» Now the clue is «about my drawing» and the word very. The teacher is saying nice things, so complimentary means praising. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then put that meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: Where you meet a word can help too. In a hotel, complimentary usually means free. At school, a complimentary comment means a kind, praising comment about your work. Read the whole sentence before you decide.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: «Complimentary» means free, given without charge, as in «complimentary drinks».
fact: «Complimentary» also means saying nice things, praising someone.
fact: A complimentary comment is a kind, praising comment about someone's work.
exercise: «The hotel gave us complimentary breakfasts every morning.» What does complimentary mean here? | Cold and wet | Praising, saying nice things | *Free, given without charge | Very large
exercise: «Her coach was very complimentary about my running.» What does complimentary mean here? | Angry and loud | *Saying nice things, praising | Free, given without charge | Quick and sudden
exercise: In the maths problem, the complimentary pens cost nothing. What does complimentary mean here? | *Free | Heavy | Expensive | Sharp
test: «She left a complimentary note saying my story was brilliant.» What does complimentary mean here? | *Praising, saying nice things | Free, given without charge | Very short | Written in pencil
test: Ben found a bag of complimentary sweets on his seat. What does complimentary mean here? | Old and broken | Praising, saying nice things | *Free, given without charge | Sticky and hot
exam: Mia's teacher wrote a complimentary comment on her poem. What does complimentary mean here? | Short and rude | Missing a line | *Praising, saying nice things | Free
